{
  "chapter": "security/least-privilege",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "Unused permissions used to be latent. An agent chooses at runtime from attacker-influenced",
    "text, so every unused grant is now reachable.",
    "Excessive agency is three excesses \u2014 permission, functionality and autonomy \u2014 and fixing",
    "one is cosmetic. Unattended execution is a permission even though it does not look like",
    "one.",
    "Six scoping axes: identity, tool, argument, time, rate, aggregate. Most implementations",
    "use only the second.",
    "Bind every write to the entities already in scope for the run, taken from your own records",
    "rather than from the model's arguments.",
    "Aggregate caps stop the slow attack that per-call caps miss: twenty small credits instead",
    "of one large one.",
    "Denials escalate rather than erroring, because a control operators disable is worth less",
    "than one that is slightly too tight.",
    "Prefer run-scoped expiring credentials, and enforce in shadow mode first so you measure",
    "the policy before it blocks anything.",
    "A dynamic tool catalogue invalidates a point-in-time audit, so a newly appeared tool is",
    "denied by default."
  ],
  "policy": {
    "requiredScopes": [
      "identity",
      "entity",
      "amount"
    ],
    "mode": "enforce"
  },
